Dock keystone, input: pointer / touch / pen hits on a dock card are mapped back
through the renderer's homography (ksHitInverse), so they land where the trapezoid is
drawn; a hit in the vacated strip beside it is a miss, not a flat-box hit. X11 dock
windows get the same mapping (keystoneSurfaceLocal).

- Edge resizing (general:resize_on_border) tests a dock/PiP card's pointer position in
  its UNWARPED frame (keystoneUnwarpGlobal), so the hover cursor follows the drawn
  trapezoid's edges instead of the flat box around it. (The press side of that test
  arrives with 10-touch-pen-border-resize, which moves it into a shared helper.)
- A pointer drag with a button held (text selection) maps through the keystone and
  scale-to-fit like the hit test, instead of the held surface's flat offset.
- Touch and pen go through the same inverse warp (keystoneUnwarpGlobal), on Wayland
  and X11 cards alike. X11 touch-down took the flat box (a tap on a tilted Wine card
  landed beside where it was drawn); touch motion on any card was the down point's
  fixed offset, which a perspective warp is not (a drag drifted off the finger); pen
  motion was flat for every card. The touch origin is kept in the unwarped frame and
  each motion point is re-mapped.

